{
  "image_name": "1e128af9af14285534e2c4204d10ea76.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts how subcutaneous fat communicates with other tissues in sedentary versus exercise-trained mice. In sedentary mice, the adipose tissue is shown mainly as white adipocytes with relatively few mitochondria. These cells release adipokines that act broadly on the liver, skeletal muscle, and brown adipose tissue.\n\nIn exercise-trained mice, the same depot appears remodeled: some cells have taken on a beige adipocyte phenotype, with a more mitochondria-rich appearance. This training-associated change in fat is linked to altered adipokine output. Instead of a broad, undefined influence, the diagram emphasizes a specific circulating “factor X” that increases glucose uptake in two metabolically active tissues: oxidative skeletal muscle and brown adipose tissue. Plus signs next to these targets indicate a positive effect.\n\nOverall, the message is that exercise changes the identity and function of subcutaneous adipose tissue, promoting beiging and a more oxidative profile. This remodeled fat then seems to send endocrine signals that improve systemic metabolism, especially by enhancing glucose handling in muscle and brown fat, compared with the more white-fat-dominated signaling pattern seen in sedentary animals.",
